    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7882675311d7ae0153200a1b2b06d8&id=8a7887a199818f4b0199a5e0260a13b7": {
      "title": "Adjunct, Political Science w/ Pre-Law Component",
      "description": "Adjunct, Political Science w/ Pre-Law Component.  Job Title: Adjunct - Political Science with Pre-Law Component Classification: Faculty Division: Academic Affairs Department: Political Science Department Reports: Chair FLSA Status: Salaried, Exempt Location: Lincoln University Main Campus ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: · We are seeking a candidate who can teach a variety of Pre-Law classes in person, such as POL 3010 – Race and American Law, POL 4000- Legal Problem Solving and Skills Development, and POL 4001- Supreme Court and Constitutional Law. The ability to also teach American National Government is preferred. · Teach in-person undergraduate courses in specific content areas as assigned by the department chairperson. · Prepares syllabi that meet department and university standards. · Prepares meaningful classroom experiences, which include a variety of pedagogical strategies, including lectures, discussion, tests and exams. · Compiles, administers, and grades examinations. · Participates in assessment activities by submitting data of student learning outcomes in a timely fashion. · In addition, all other relevant duties as assigned by the Chair. EDUCATION: · J.D. degree in related field required. J.D. / Ph.D. preferred. EXPERIENCE: · Some teaching experience as the instructor of record in an undergraduate/graduate institution is required. · The candidate must be an Attorney at Law and conversant with law school admission requirements and how to prepare students for entry and success in law school. QUALIFICATIONS: · Must have a J.D. · Must have teaching experience. · Must be able to work with little or no supervision. · Must be able to make decisions supporting the department’s mission and goals. · Must have excellent communication skills, oral and written. · Must have interpersonal skills, and the ability to work within a culturally diverse organization. · Must have a high level of professional appearance and demeanor. · Must have ability to maintain a high level of confidentiality. WORK ENVIRONMENT: Work is primarily performed in a university campus environment and requires a flexible schedule including possible evening and weekend work. The employee is subject to inside environmental conditions, protection from weather conditions but not necessarily from temperature changes. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Moderate lifting up to 20 pounds as frequently as needed to move objects; dexterity to write and manipulate computer keyboard and mouse; ability to hear and speak clearly; and body mobility to stoop, kneel, and bend and reach. APPLICATION PROCEDURES: · Applicants must submit the following: · A current curriculum vitae or resume that includes information on education, experience, and ability related to the position. · Copies of all college/university transcripts verifying degree(s) and/or coursework. · The names and contact information for (3) three references. Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7882675311d7ae0153200a1b2b06d8&id=8a78839f8657c7ab01865b0d8f7553e1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Psychology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Psychology.  JOB TITLE: Assistant Professor of Psychology CLASSIFICATION: Faculty DIVISION: Academic Affairs DEPARTMENT: Department of Psychology and Human Services LOCATION: Lincoln University, PA, Main Campus (This is an in-person position) FLSA STATUS: Exempt REPORTS TO: SALARY Department Chair PER CBA OVERVIEW: Lincoln University (LU), a historically Black, regional, comprehensive, liberal arts university is one of the 4 state-related institutions within the Pennsylvania Higher Education System. The university enrolls over 2,000 students, employs more than 100 full-time faculty members, and offers more than 30 undergraduate programs, and selected graduate programs. JOB SUMMARY: Lincoln University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position in the Department of Psychology and Human Services. Preference will be given to applicants with a specialization in Educational and or Social Psychology. The successful candidate will be a dynamic teacher of undergraduate students as well as a scholar with a dedicated research and publication agenda with evidence of thoughtful intellectual engagement with Psychology. Applicants skilled in contemporary pedagogical methods various teaching formats as well as interest in curricular development as well as outcomes assessments and program review are highly desirable. The successful applicant will also be expected to engage in research endeavors that would interest and involve undergraduate students as well as to participate in departmental and university initiatives and activities. The successful candidate must possess a Ph.D./Terminal Degree by the hire date. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: The successful candidate will: Offer dynamic, engaging, and student-centered pedagogy utilizing up-to-date expertise in Psychology and the best pedagogical practices in Psychology. Will teach a normal load, typically four courses in-person a semester, including general education classes and other required and elective courses in the psychology major. Will be a contributing member of departmental and university required meetings and initiatives including departmental meetings events, course and curriculum development, and university-wide initiatives. Maintain active professional engagement and research, which are required for promotion and tenure consideration. Will share the University’s commitment to diversity, inclusion, and anti-racist pedagogy and be prepared to join an active cohort of faculty who teach undergraduate courses and advise/mentor students. EDUCATION: A Ph.D./Terminal degree in Psychology completed by the time of hire. EXPERIENCE: Evidence of 1+ years of teaching at undergraduate level Proven agenda of research and professional engagement Communication skills consistent with effective teaching, mentoring and motivating Psychology majors from a broad range of cultural, ethnic, and economic backgrounds and actively participating in the department and broader university. Experience in course design, development, and assessment of student learning out comes. Experience utilizing LMS such as canvas and other software PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Moderate lifting up to 20 pounds as frequently as needed to move objects; dexterity to write and manipulate computer keyboard and mouse; ability to hear and speak clearly; and body mobility to stoop, kneel, bend and reach. WORK ENVIRONMENT: Work is performed in a university campus environment. All classes are currently taught in-person and faculty are required to be present on campus to teach their courses. The employee is subject to inside environmental conditions and protection from weather conditions. APPLICATION PROCEDURES: All applications should be submitted through the online portal. Only complete applications will be considered. Applicants must submit the following: A letter of application A current curriculum vita Undergraduate and graduate transcripts [unofficial copies will suffice for initial screening]. A statement of teaching philosophy A research statement A diversity statement that discusses the incorporation of the principles of diversity into your teaching, research, and service Three [3] references or three [3] current and relevant letters of recommendation Review of applications will continue until position is filled. Lincoln University is an equal opportunity employer and encourages all qualified individuals to apply. The University is committed to enriching its educational experience and culture by fostering a dynamic community of faculty, administration, and staff Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7882675311d7ae0153200a1b2b06d8&id=8a7882675311d7ae01532f7164c70a86": {
      "title": "Campus Police Officer (FT)",
      "description": "Campus Police Officer (FT).  Job Title: Campus Police Officer Classification: Service Workers Division: Office of the President Department: Public Safety Reports to: Director of Public Safety & Chief of Police FLSA Status: Hourly, Non-Exempt Lincoln University Located on 422 acres in southern Chester County Pennsylvania, Lincoln University serves approximately 2,000 undergraduates and nearly 250 graduate students from more than 25 states and 15 countries. Since its founding in 1854, Lincoln University has been internationally recognized for its commitment to excellence in scholarship and service. Lincoln University graduates have distinguished themselves in many fields including medicine, science, technology, education, law, and the arts. Notable among Lincoln University graduates are Supreme Court Justice Thurgood Marshall ’30; poet and playwright Langston Hughes ’29; the first president of Nigeria, Nnamdi Azikiwe ’30; the first president of Ghana, Kwame Nkrumah ’39; the first African American female Navy rear admiral, Lillian Fishburne ’71; and the first female lieutenant governor of New Jersey, Sheila Oliver ’74. Lincoln University was initially chartered as Ashmun Institute in honor of Jehudi Ashmun, the first governor of Liberia, and renamed Lincoln University in 1866 after the assassination of President Abraham Lincoln. The University first admitted women students in 1952 and formally associated with the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania in 1972 as a state-related, co-educational University. Lincoln University is accredited by the Middle States Commission on Higher Education. JOB SUMMARY: Lincoln University seeks Campus Police Officers. This position reports to the Director of Public Safety & Security and will be responsible for securing exterior doors after visitation hours and frequently making tours of the residence halls. They will be responsible for identifying and escorting members of the opposite sex out of the building and informing the Residence Hall Coordinator in writing. This position is responsible for making sure all rules and regulations are followed and assisting with fire drills and investigating false alarms. This position must maintain a good working relationship with security and inform them of any illegal activities that may occur during the night shift.​ ESSENTIAL DUITES & RESPONSIBILITIES: Supervises the general facilities. Conducts tours frequently throughout the residence halls. Secures all exterior and emergency doors. Ensures that all policies, rules, and regulations are being followed (i.e., visitation, smoking, quiet hours, etc.). Reports any concerns to the Residence Hall Coordinator (i.e., maintenance, false alarms, security, etc.). Ensures student participation in fire drills/false alarms. Maintains contact with security in case of an emergency. Submits all paperwork in a timely manner. EDUCATION: High school diploma or GED required. QUALIFICATIONS: Law enforcement or security training preferred, but not required. CPR and first aid certification preferred, but not required. Must have valid driver's license and own transportation. Must have and maintain a clean criminal record. Flexible schedule and weekend hours included. Must communicate effectively with people from varied social, educational, and economic backgrounds. Must be able to work without constant supervision, make decisions, and have good judgment while performing under stress. Must be able to handle multiple tasks and projects and prioritize based on department functionality. Must have the ability to properly operate and care for computerized dispatch system and voice radio equipment. Must possess the ability to learn appropriate computer functions. Must have the ability to keep concise and accurate records and carry out verbal instructions. Must have the ability to write complete and concise reports. Must maintain emotional stability and physical condition necessary to perform related duties. Must maintain a high level of confidentiality. Must have a high level of professional appearance and demeanor, wear a standard issue security uniform, and carry standard security equipment. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Moderate lifting up to 20 pounds as frequently as needed to move objects; dexterity to write and manipulate computer keyboard and mouse; ability to hear and speak clearly; and body mobility to stoop, kneel, and bend and reach. WORK ENVIRONMENT: Work is primarily performed in a university campus environment and requires a flexible schedule including evening and weekend work. Travel for administrative work is expected. The employee is subject to inside environmental conditions: protection from weather conditions but not necessarily from temperature changes. Lincoln University is a equal opportunity employer and encourages all qualified individuals to apply. The University is committed to enriching it's educational experience and culture by fostering a dynamic and inclusive faculty, administration, and staff. Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7882675311d7ae0153200a1b2b06d8&id=8a7882675311d7ae01532f7943c60a8f": {
      "title": "Campus Security Officer (FT)",
      "description": "Campus Security Officer (FT).  Job title: Campus Security Officer Department: Public Safety Reports to: Director of Public Safety Type: Full-time/Regular Application Deadline: Open Until Filled JOB SUMMARY: Lincoln University seeks Campus Security Officers. This position reports to the Director of Public Safety & Security and will be responsible for securing exterior doors after visitation hours and frequently making tours of the residence halls. They will be responsible for identifying and escorting members of the opposite sex out of the building and informing the Residence Hall Coordinator in writing. This position is responsible for making sure all rules and regulations are followed and assisting with fire drills and investigating false alarms. This position must maintain a good working relationship with security and inform them of any illegal activities that may occur during the night shift.​ ESSENTIAL DUITES & RESPONSIBILITIES: Supervises the general facilities. Conducts tours frequently throughout the residence halls. Secures all exterior and emergency doors. Ensures that all policies, rules, and regulations are being followed (i.e., visitation, smoking, quiet hours, etc.). Reports any concerns to the Residence Hall Coordinator (i.e., maintenance, false alarms, security, etc.). Ensures student participation in fire drills/false alarms. Maintains contact with security in case of an emergency. Submits all paperwork in a timely manner. EDUCATION: High school diploma or GED required. QUALIFICATIONS: Law enforcement or security training preferred, but not required. CPR and first aid certification preferred, but not required. Must have valid driver's license and own transportation. Must have and maintain a clean criminal record. Flexible schedule and weekend hours included. Must communicate effectively with people from varied social, educational, and economic backgrounds. Must be able to work without constant supervision, make decisions, and have good judgment while performing under stress. Must be able to handle multiple tasks and projects and prioritize based on department functionality. Must have the ability to properly operate and care for computerized dispatch system and voice radio equipment. Must possess the ability to learn appropriate computer functions. Must have the ability to keep concise and accurate records and carry out verbal instructions. Must have the ability to write complete and concise reports. Must maintain emotional stability and physical condition necessary to perform related duties. Must maintain a high level of confidentiality. Must have a high level of professional appearance and demeanor, wear a standard issue security uniform, and carry standard security equipment. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Moderate lifting up to 20 pounds as frequently as needed to move objects; dexterity to write and manipulate computer keyboard and mouse; ability to hear and speak clearly; and body mobility to stoop, kneel, and bend and reach. WORK ENVIRONMENT: Work is primarily performed in a university campus environment and requires a flexible schedule including evening and weekend work. Travel for administrative work is expected. The employee is subject to inside environmental conditions: protection from weather conditions but not necessarily from temperature changes. Lincoln University is a equal opportunity employer and encourages all qualified individuals to apply. The University is committed to enriching it's educational experience and culture by fostering a dynamic and inclusive faculty, administration, and staff. Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7882675311d7ae0153200a1b2b06d8&id=8a7883ac9e6b0bdf019e8df1e3d17abd": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor – Criminal Justice (Part-Time)",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor – Criminal Justice (Part-Time).  Lincoln University invites applications for a part-time Adjunct Instructor position in the Department of Anthropology, Sociology, and Criminal Justice for the Fall 2026 semester. This is an in-person teaching position on Lincoln University's main campus in Lincoln University, Pennsylvania. The successful candidate will teach one or more in-person undergraduate Criminal Justice courses, including: Crime Scene Investigation (Forensic Science) Criminal Investigation Responsibilities Teach assigned undergraduate Criminal Justice courses. Prepare and deliver course content consistent with departmental learning outcomes. Assess student learning and maintain accurate records of student performance. Hold appropriate office hours and communicate effectively with students. Submit grades and course-related materials in accordance with university policies. Minimum Qualifications Master's degree in Criminal Justice, Criminology, Forensic Science, Public Administration, Sociology, or a closely related field from an accredited institution. Demonstrated expertise in criminal investigations, forensic science, crime scene investigation, law enforcement, or a related area. Preferred Qualifications College-level teaching experience. Professional experience in law enforcement, criminal investigations, forensic science, crime scene processing, corrections, or related criminal justice fields. Experience working with diverse student populations. Lincoln University, the nation's first degree-granting Historically Black College and University (HBCU), is committed to fostering academic excellence and student success. Application Materials Applicants should submit: Cover letter Curriculum vitae or resume Unofficial transcripts (official transcripts required upon hire) Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7882675311d7ae0153200a1b2b06d8&id=8a78839f9b1376bd019b276e94b12b1a": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor in Anthropology",
      "description": "Adjunct Professor in Anthropology.  JOB TITLE: Adjunct Professor in Anthropology CLASSIFICATION: Faculty DIVISION: Academic Affairs DEPARTMENT: Department of Anthropology, Sociology, and Criminal Justice LOCATION: Lincoln University, PA, Main Campus FLSA STATUS: Exempt REPORTS TO: Chair, Anthropology, Sociology, and Criminal Justice SALARY: PER CBA OVERVIEW: Lincoln University (LU), a Black, regional, comprehensive, liberal arts university is a state-related institution within the Pennsylvania Higher Education System. The university enrolls over 2,000 students, employs more than 100 full-time faculty members, and offers more than 30 undergraduate programs, and selected graduate programs. SUMMARY: This is a part-time, in-person opportunity, for an Adjunct Professor to facilitate undergraduate courses in the field of Anthropology. A successful candidate should possess the ability to engage thought, encourage questions, and guide meaningful application of content presented within the classroom both to individual students and the collective group of learners. This position would include a range of subject matters in the discipline of Anthropology. ESSENTIAL RESPONSIBILITIES: Teach in-person undergraduate courses as assigned by the Department Chair, in the discipline of Anthropology. Prepare syllabi according to the University/Department guidelines provided. Compile, administer, and grade all homework assignments, projects, papers, and exams needed to promote comprehensive learning of the subject matter(s) presented. Present course content, assignments, and other supporting materials relevant to the course on the Learning Management System (LMS) to allow for easy student access. Provide multiple graded artifacts (assignments, projects, exams) for the assessment of the course as needed. QUALIFICATIONS: Master’s degree (or higher) in the Anthropology discipline required. Prior teaching experience in a University/College setting preferred. Excellent communication skills (verbal and written). WORK ENVIRONMENT: Work is primarily performed in a university campus environment and requires a flexible schedule. Occasional travel for conferences of other departmental administrative work is expected. The employee is subject to inside environmental conditions - protection from weather conditions but not necessarily from temperature changes. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Moderate lifting up to 20 pounds as frequently as needed to move objects; dexterity to write and manipulate computer keyboard and mouse; ability to hear and speak clearly; and body mobility to stoop, kneel, bend and reach. APPLICATION GUIDELINES: All applications should be submitted through the online portal. Only complete applications will be considered. Applicants must submit the following: Letter of interest from applicant. Curriculum vitae that includes education, experience, and other contributions/awards relevant to this position. Copies of all college/university transcripts verifying completion and coursework studied. Provide (3) professional reference letters. Lincoln University is a equal opportunity employer and encourages all qualified individuals to apply. The University is committed to enriching it's educational experience and culture by fostering a dynamic and inclusive faculty, administration, and staff. Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7882675311d7ae0153200a1b2b06d8&id=8a7885aca03fc28401a05e8da7aa1a7e": {
      "title": "Assistant Criminal Justice Professor",
      "description": "Assistant Criminal Justice Professor.  JOB TITLE: Assistant Professor, Criminal Justice CLASSIFICATION: Faculty DIVISION: Academic Affairs DEPARTMENT: Department of Anthropology, Sociology, and Criminal Justice LOCATION: Lincoln University, PA, Main Campus FLSA STATUS: Exempt REPORTS TO: SALARY Chair, Anthropology, Sociology, and Criminal Justice PER CBA OVERVIEW: Lincoln University (LU), a historically Black, regional, comprehensive, liberal arts university is one of the 17 state-related institutions within the Pennsylvania Higher Education System. The university enrolls over 2,000 students, employs more than 100 full-time faculty members, and offers more than 30 undergraduate programs, and selected graduate programs. Diversity is at the core of LU’s history, mission, and values. We understand the value of diversifying our faculty and pursue that standard of excellence by focusing our recruitment efforts to attract candidates with rich and varied backgrounds, scholarship, and experience. JOB SUMMARY: Lincoln University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position in Criminal Justice. The successful candidate will be an engaging teacher of undergraduate students and an active scholar with a developing research and publication agenda. Candidates should demonstrate thoughtful intellectual engagement with the field of Criminal Justice, expertise in law and the legal system, and a commitment to student success. The teaching load is five courses per academic year and includes in-person instruction in courses such as Introduction to Criminal Justice, Criminal Law, Courts and Corrections, Research Methods, and upper-level Criminal Justice electives. Candidates must have demonstrated expertise in law, courts, legal studies, or a closely related area. The department is particularly interested in candidates who will contribute to experiential learning opportunities and collaborate with faculty and students in developing a mock trial team. The successful candidate must possess a Ph.D. in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or a closely related field by the date of appointment. Candidates who also hold a J.D. are especially encouraged to apply. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: The successful candidate will: Offer dynamic, engaging, and student-centered pedagogy utilizing up-to-date expertise in Criminal Justice and the best pedagogical practices. Will teach a normal load, typically five courses in-person a year, including general education classes, Criminological Theory, Criminal Law, Courts, Corrections, Criminal Investigation, and Crime Scene Investigation. Actively mentor and advise students. Will be a contributing member of departmental and university required meetings and initiatives including departmental meetings events, course and curriculum development, and university-wide initiatives. Maintain active professional engagement and research, which are required for promotion and tenure consideration. Will share the University’s commitment to diversity, inclusion, and anti-racist pedagogy and be prepared to join an active cohort of faculty who teach undergraduate courses and advise/mentor students. EDUCATION: A Ph.D. in Criminal Justice, Criminology, Law, or a closely related field by the time of appointment. ABDs will be considered if the Ph.D. is completed by the time of hire. EXPERIENCE: Evidence of 1+ years of teaching at undergraduate level Proven agenda of research and professional engagement Communication skills consistent with effective teaching, mentoring and motivating Criminal Justice majors from a broad range of cultural, ethnic, and economic backgrounds and actively participating in the department and broader university. Experience in course design, development, and assessment of student learning outcomes. Experience utilizing LMS such as Canvas PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Moderate lifting up to 20 pounds as frequently as needed to move objects; dexterity to write and manipulate computer keyboard and mouse; ability to hear and speak clearly; and body mobility to stoop, kneel, bend and reach. WORK ENVIRONMENT: Work is performed in a university campus environment. All classes are currently taught in-person and faculty are required to be present on campus to teach their courses. The employee is subject to environmental conditions and protection from weather conditions. APPLICATION PROCEDURES: All applications should be submitted through the online portal. Only complete applications will be considered. Applicants must submit the following: A letter of application A current curriculum vita Undergraduate and graduate transcripts [unofficial copies will suffice for initial screening]. Three [3] references or three [3] current and relevant letters of recommendation Review of applications will begin September 20th, 2026, and continue until position is filled. Women and underrepresented groups are especially encouraged to apply. Lincoln University is an equal opportunity employer and encourages women, people of color, individuals with disabilities, and veterans to apply. The University is committed to enriching its educational experience and its culture through the diversity of its faculty, administration, and staff. Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7882675311d7ae0153200a1b2b06d8&id=8a7885aca03fc28401a05e7faae00a81": {
      "title": "Assistant Sociology Professor",
      "description": "Assistant Sociology Professor.  JOB TITLE: Assistant Professor, Sociology CLASSIFICATION: Faculty DIVISION: Academic Affairs DEPARTMENT: Department of Anthropology, Sociology, and Criminal Justice LOCATION: Lincoln University, PA, Main Campus FLSA STATUS: Exempt REPORTS TO: SALARY Chair, Anthropology, Sociology, and Criminal Justice PER CBA OVERVIEW: Lincoln University (LU), a historically Black, regional, comprehensive, liberal arts university is one of the 17 state-related institutions within the Pennsylvania Higher Education System. The university enrolls over 2,000 students, employs more than 100 full-time faculty members, offers more than 30 undergraduate programs, and selected graduate programs. Diversity is at the core of LU’s history, mission, and values. We understand the value of diversifying our faculty and pursue that standard of excellence by focusing our recruitment efforts to attract candidates with rich and varied backgrounds, scholarships, and experience. JOB SUMMARY: Lincoln University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position in Sociology. The successful candidate will be an engaging teacher of undergraduate students and an active scholar with a developing research and publication agenda. Candidates should demonstrate thoughtful intellectual engagement with the discipline of Sociology and a commitment to student success. The teaching load is five courses per academic year and includes in-person instruction in courses such as Research Methods, Sociological Theory, Social Statistics, and upper-level Sociology electives. Areas of specialization are open, though candidates with interests that complement existing departmental offerings are especially encouraged to apply. The successful candidate must possess a Ph.D. or terminal degree by the date of appointment. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: The successful candidate will: Teach undergraduate Sociology courses using engaging, student-centered pedagogy and current disciplinary knowledge. Teach a standard load of five in-person courses per academic year, including general education and upper-level Sociology courses. Advise and mentor undergraduate students. Participate in departmental, college, and university service, including meetings, curriculum development, assessment activities, and university initiatives. Maintain an active research and professional engagement agenda consistent with expectations for tenure and promotion. Demonstrate a commitment to effective teaching and student engagement in a diverse learning environment. EDUCATION: A Ph.D. in Sociology or a closely related field by the time of appointment. ABDs will be considered if the Ph.D. is completed by the time of hire. EXPERIENCE: Evidence of undergraduate teaching experience Active research and professional engagement agenda Strong communication skills and demonstrated ability to effectively teach, mentor, and support students from diverse educational and cultural backgrounds Experience in course design, curriculum development, and assessment of student learning outcomes Experience utilizing LMS platforms such as Canvas PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Moderate lifting up to 20 pounds as frequently as needed to move objects; dexterity to write and manipulate computer keyboard and mouse; ability to hear and speak clearly; and body mobility to stoop, kneel, bend and reach. WORK ENVIRONMENT: Work is performed in a university campus environment. All classes are currently taught in-person and faculty are required to be present on campus to teach their courses. The employee is subject to environmental conditions and protection from weather conditions. APPLICATION PROCEDURES: All applications should be submitted through the online portal. Only complete applications will be considered. Applicants must submit the following: A letter of application A current curriculum vita Undergraduate and graduate transcripts [unofficial copies will suffice for initial screening]. Three [3] references or three [3] current and relevant letters of recommendation Review of applications will begin September 20th, 2026, and continue until position is filled. Women and underrepresented groups are especially encouraged to apply. Lincoln University is an equal opportunity employer and encourages women, people of color, individuals with disabilities, and veterans to apply. The University is committed to enriching its educational experience and its culture through the diversity of its faculty, administration, and staff. Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7882675311d7ae0153200a1b2b06d8&id=8a7883a8818179800181b0f93faf6790": {
      "title": "Program Assistant",
      "description": "Program Assistant.  Job Title: Program Assistant FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Salary: Hourly Rate — 37.5 hours per week Reports To: Department Administrator or Designee JOB SUMMARY: Within established department procedural guidelines, the incumbent performs advanced or complex administrative support functions to complete assigned duties and ensure compliance with University policies and external/governmental agency regulations. Essential job functions may include, but are not limited to the following: ESSENTIAL DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES: Plan and coordinate office workflow to meet departmental performance objectives and ensure operational efficiency. Perform advanced data and document processing functions in automated systems. Prepare and distribute general correspondence and financial reports. Respond to walk-in, e-mail and telephone inquiries and resolve related issues. Maintain an organized office and protect sensitive/confidential information. Access, retrieve and store records according to established file systems. Perform general business and banking transactions, i.e., manage cash receipts, prepare bank deposits, purchase orders, account reconciliation, and accounts and receivable. Compile and analyze financial and/or statistical data. Prepare and distribute general correspondence and financial reports. Complete special or cyclical projects and additional task that reflect the nature of the actual work assignment. EDUCATION: 60+ college credits, Associate’s degree, specialized training or certification in related area, or five or more years of related and commendable work experience. EXPERIENCE: Demonstrated advanced working knowledge of finance procedures and systems. Outstanding analytical, technical, multi-tasking, time management, organization, and interpersonal skills. Exceptional ability to build and maintain effective working relationships. Exceptional data entry and processing skills. Proficiency in automated financial / records management and document processing systems, i.e., MS Office Suite of Applications. Work well independently and in a team environment. Demonstrated ability to use good judgment, follow through on assigned tasks, and maintain confidentiality. Commitment to professional development. WORK ENVIRONMENT: Work is primarily performed in a university campus environment and requires a flexible schedule including evening and weekend work. Travel for administrative work is expected. The employee is subject to inside environmental conditions: protection from weather conditions but not necessarily from temperature changes. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Moderate lifting up to 20 pounds as frequently as needed to move objects; dexterity to write and manipulate computer keyboard and mouse; ability to hear and speak clearly; and body mobility to stoop, kneel, bend and reach. Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7882675311d7ae0153200a1b2b06d8&id=8a78839ea06420ea01a0a754179e672b": {
      "title": "STEM & Health Profession Pathways Director",
      "description": "STEM & Health Profession Pathways Director.  Job Title: STEM & Health Profession Pathways Director Classification: Professional Division: Student Success Department: Center for Career Success and Experiential Learning Reports To: Executive Director FLSA Status: Exempt Location: Lincoln University, Pa Job Summary The STEM & Health Profession Pathways Director should function as the university's pipeline architect for STEM careers and advanced education. Rather than simply advising students, the director intentionally builds partnerships, creates experiential learning opportunities, coordinates research and professional development, and equips students with the experiences, competencies, and networks needed to succeed in competitive STEM and health professions. Working collaboratively with the other offices within the Center for Career Success & Experiential Learning, the director ensures students have access to an integrated pathway from exploration to graduate study or career placement. A STEM & Health Profession Pathways Director serves as the bridge between academic preparation, experiential learning, and post-graduation opportunities for students pursuing careers in science, technology, engineering, mathematics, and health professions. The role extends beyond academic advising by creating structured pathways that prepare students for graduate education, professional schools, research, internships, and employment. Essential Duties & Responsibilities Strategic Leadership Develop and implement a comprehensive STEM and Health Professions strategic plan aligned with institutional priorities. Establish measurable goals for student participation, retention, graduate school acceptance, and career outcomes. Collaborate with academic departments to identify workforce trends and emerging STEM fields. Represent the university at regional and national STEM and health professions consortiums. Student Advising and Pathway Development Advise students interested in careers in medicine, dentistry, pharmacy, nursing, physician assistant, veterinary medicine, public health, engineering, computer science, biotechnology, and other STEM fields. Assist students in developing individualized academic and career plans. Guide students through prerequisite planning, competitive applicant preparation, and professional school admissions processes. Conduct workshops on application timelines, personal statements, interviews, and entrance examinations (e.g., MCAT, DAT, GRE, PA-CAT). Experiential Learning Coordinate research, clinical, internship, shadowing, and service-learning opportunities. Develop relationships with hospitals, laboratories, research institutions, biotechnology companies, government agencies, and STEM employers. Expand opportunities for undergraduate research and faculty-mentored projects. Support students in obtaining paid experiential learning opportunities and grants. Graduate and Professional School Preparation Prepare students for admission to graduate and professional schools. Coordinate mock interviews and admissions preparation. Review personal statements and application materials. Facilitate committee letter processes where applicable. Monitor acceptance rates and student outcomes Partnership Development Establish articulation agreements and early assurance partnerships with medical schools, graduate programs, and healthcare organizations. Develop partnerships with federal agencies, research institutions, and corporate STEM organizations. Coordinate employer engagement activities specific to STEM industries. Cultivate alumni mentors working in STEM and healthcare careers. Programming and Events Develop annual programming such as: STEM Career Exploration Week Health Professions Symposium Research Showcase Graduate School Preparation Series MCAT/GRE preparation workshops Industry site visits Faculty and alumni panel discussions Summer research opportunities information sessions Clinical experience fairs Student Success Initiatives Identify and support students from entry through graduation. Monitor academic progress and intervene with at-risk students. Promote participation in high-impact practices. Encourage leadership development within STEM organizations. Coordinate peer mentoring initiatives. Research and Scholarship Support Assist students in identifying research opportunities. Support conference presentation submissions. Promote undergraduate publication opportunities. Help students secure research funding and travel grants. Collaborate with the Undergraduate Research office to increase participation Data Collection and Assessment Track and analyze metrics such as: Student participation Internship placements Research participation Clinical hours completed Graduate/professional school applications Acceptance rates Scholarship awards Employer partnerships Student learning outcomes Program effectiveness Marketing and Outreach Recruit students into STEM and health professions initiatives. Develop promotional materials highlighting opportunities. Maintain a website and social media presence. Coordinate outreach to prospective students and families. Showcase student and alumni success stories. Grant Development Identify external funding opportunities. Assist in writing grant proposals. Manage STEM program grants. Coordinate reporting requirements for funded initiatives. Collaboration Across the Center for Career Success & Experiential Learning Partner closely with the other offices to create seamless student experience Minimum Qualifications Master’s degree Allied Health, Health Sciences and/or Organizational Development Four (4) years of related work experience. Experience managing start-up programs in Higher Education Environment Proven ability to work with multiple departments (union and administration) Ability to use a variety of software, databases and application platforms Excellent interpersonal skills and demonstrated ability to work in a collaborative environment Excellent oral and written communication skills Preferred Qualifications • PhD or equivalent in life, physical, or biomedical sciences or a related field. • Five (5) years of advising, teaching, mentoring, and/or administrative experience with an emphasis on undergraduate student programming and interaction in a university setting. • Experience with national fellowships/research sponsors • Grantsmanship experience • Experience working with faculty, administrators, and other university colleagues • Experience working with first generation, underrepresented minority, and underserved populations • Familiarity with HBCU institutions WORK ENVIRONMENT: Work is primarily performed in a university campus environment and requires a flexible schedule including possible evening and weekend work. The employee is subject to environmental conditions, protection from weather conditions but not necessarily from temperature changes. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Moderate lifting up to 20 pounds as frequently as needed to move objects; dexterity to write and manipulate computer keyboard and mouse; ability to hear and speak clearly; and body mobility to stoop, kneel, bend and reach DISCLAIMER: The incumbent must perform the essential duties and responsibilities with or without reasonable accommodation. The above statements are intended to describe the general nature and level of work being performed by employees assigned to this classification. They are not intended to be construed as an exhaustive list of all responsibilities, duties and /or skills required. This job description is not an employment agreement and /or an expressed or implied contract. Management has the right to alter this job description at any time without notice. Lincoln University is an equal opportunity employer and encourages all qualified individuals to apply. The University is committed to enriching its educational experience and culture by fostering a dynamic community of faculty, administration, and staff. Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7882675311d7ae0153200a1b2b06d8&id=8a7887a1a064211201a09c9dd6ff68b6": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Title III",
      "description": "Assistant Director, Title III.  TITLE III ASSISTANT DIRECTOR LINCOLN UNIVERSITY (PA) Position Summary The Title III Assistant Director reports to and provides strategic administrative, programmatic, fiscal, compliance, and grant-management support to the Title III Director and Executive Director in administering the University’s federally funded Title III grant program. The position supports planning, implementation, monitoring, documentation, evaluation, reporting, and compliance with U.S. Department of Education requirements, federal cost principles, Uniform Guidance, and University policies and procedures.The Assistant Director oversees day-to-day grant administration and serves as a key liaison among Activity Directors, faculty, staff, administrators, and campus stakeholders. The position provides guidance on program requirements, fiscal procedures, documentation, reporting, compliance, and grant administration; coordinates multiple priorities and deadlines; and supports institutional capacity, student success, academic and administrative effectiveness, and sustainability consistent with the mission of HBCUs. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Grant Administration, Federal Systems & Compliance • Assist with overall administration, implementation, and monitoring of the Title III grant and preparation/submission of federal grant materials through Grants.gov and other designated systems. • Monitor activities for compliance with DOE regulations, federal statutes, grant terms and conditions, applicable cost principles, Uniform Guidance, and University policies. • Interpret and communicate federal requirements, Title III regulations, guidance, and grant conditions; support policies, procedures, and internal controls. • Identify compliance, performance, or fiscal concerns and promptly communicate them to the Title III Director and Executive Director; maintain secure records and required retention systems. Program Planning, Coordination & Implementation • Coordinate with Activity Directors and campus stakeholders to implement approved projects; track milestones, deliverables, timelines, performance measures, and documentation. • Ensure funded activities remain consistent with approved narratives, objectives, performance plans, budgets, and institutional priorities; participate in assigned meetings, committees, workshops, conferences, and institutional advancement activities. Fiscal & Budget Oversight • Track Title III budgets and expenditures; review and reconcile monthly financial reports and verify accuracy, allowability, allocability, and reasonableness of transactions. • Assist with budget proposals, revisions, financial statements, expenditure reports, and annual grant reports. Reporting, Assessment & Accountability • Assist with required University and DOE reports, including Title III Phase I and Phase II reports, annual performance/progress reports, internal assessments, and supporting documentation. • Monitor reporting deadlines and maintain accurate records of activities, accomplishments, expenditures, performance outcomes, and documentation. Personnel, Property & Grant Records • Maintain accurate time-and-effort documentation; assist with inventory of equipment/property purchased with Title III funds and maintain supporting records. • Protect confidential grant, financial, personnel, and institutional information; maintain organized, accessible, and secure electronic/physical files sufficient for reporting, audits, monitoring reviews, accountability, and closeout. Training, Communication & Outreach • Develop and present informational materials, workshops, and training for faculty, staff, administrators, and other members of the University community. • Support the Title III website and communication platforms; promote awareness of Title III initiatives and their contributions to institutional effectiveness, student success, and the HBCU mission; participate in professional development and Title III training. HBCU Mission & Institutional Advocacy • Demonstrate knowledge of HBCU history, mission, priorities, and institutional needs and support maximizing Title III resources for academic quality, student achievement, technology, infrastructure, administrative capacity, institutional effectiveness, sustainability, and approved priorities. Minimum Qualifications • Bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution in business administration, accounting, finance, public administration, education, higher education administration, or related field; master’s preferred. • 3–5 years of progressively responsible experience in grant administration, sponsored programs, fiscal management, higher education administration, compliance, or related administrative environment preferred. • Knowledge of federal grant administration, sponsored programs, financial management, compliance, budget monitoring, reconciliation, reporting, and documentation; ability to interpret and apply regulations and policies. • Demonstrated interest in and commitment to HBCU objectives, mission, and institutional advancement. • Strong organizational, analytical, critical-thinking, problem-solving, decision-making, written, verbal, interpersonal, and presentation skills; ability to manage multiple priorities and maintain confidentiality. • Technical proficiency with Microsoft Office, particularly Excel, and applicable University financial, ERP, student information, and grant-management systems such as Ellucian and Self-Service. Working Conditions Fast-paced, collaborative higher education environment requiring professionalism, discretion, accuracy, sound judgment, and frequent interaction with faculty, staff, administrators, Activity Directors, financial/administrative offices, federal agencies, and other stakeholders. Occasional evening, weekend, or travel responsibilities may be required for grant meetings, training, conferences, audits, monitoring reviews, professional development, or University activities. This is a grant-funded position essential to effective Title III administration, compliance, fiscal stewardship, program Apply for this Position Apply with Indeed Go back to the job list powered by"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a7882675311d7ae0153200a1b2b06d8&id=8a7887a1a150293901a15f14e73a20d6": {
      "title": "Associate Vice President, Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Coordinator",
      "description": "Associate Vice President, Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Coordinator.  Associate Vice President, Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Coordinator Classification: Administrative Division: Legal Affairs Department: Office of Civil Rights (renamed from Office of Institutional Equities) Reports To: Vice President, General Counsel & Secretary to the University FLSA Status: Salaried, Exempt Job Type: Regular Full-Time / 12 Months Location: Lincoln University, PA Campus: Main Campus POSITION SUMMARY The Associate Vice President, Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Coordinator provides administrative and operational leadership for Lincoln University’s Office of Civil Rights within the Office of the General Counsel. Reporting directly to the Vice President, General Counsel and Secretary to the University, the Associate Vice President is responsible for the daily leadership, management, coordination, and effective administration of the University’s civil rights compliance programs. The Associate Vice President oversees an integrated compliance framework encompassing Title IX, Title VI, coordination of Title VII compliance, the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, Accessibility Services, digital accessibility, discrimination and harassment prevention, investigations, complaint resolution, regulatory compliance, and institutional policy administration. The position serves as the University’s designated Title IX Coordinator and Title VI Coordinator and provides operational oversight of personnel and programs responsible for accessibility services and other assigned civil rights compliance functions. Working under the executive oversight and strategic direction of the Vice President and General Counsel, the Associate Vice President implements institutional compliance priorities, establishes operational procedures, supervises personnel, monitors regulatory obligations, coordinates institutional responses to civil rights concerns, and identifies and escalates significant compliance risks. The Associate Vice President works collaboratively with University leadership, faculty, staff, students, and external regulatory agencies to promote compliance with applicable federal and state civil rights laws and ensure fair, consistent, timely, and effective administration of the University’s civil rights responsibilities. The position requires substantial knowledge of higher education civil rights law, demonstrated administrative leadership, sound judgment, and the ability to manage complex and sensitive institutional matters while maintaining appropriate impartiality, confidentiality, and procedural fairness. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES 1. Office Leadership, Administration, and Operations Direct and manage the daily operations of the Office of Civil Rights, ensuring effective administration of assigned institutional civil rights compliance functions. Implement strategic priorities, operational objectives, and compliance initiatives established in coordination with the Vice President and General Counsel. Supervise, evaluate, mentor, and support professional and administrative personnel assigned to the Office of Civil Rights, including Accessibility Services and other designated functions. Establish and maintain operational procedures, workflow standards, performance measures, and accountability mechanisms to promote efficiency, consistency, and timely service delivery. Oversee office staffing, resource utilization, budget administration, and operational planning in accordance with University policies and established authority. Develop annual operational goals and assess office performance against institutional compliance priorities. Provide regular reports and recommendations to the Vice President and General Counsel regarding office operations, compliance trends, resource needs, significant matters, and opportunities for improvement. Coordinate with University divisions and departments to facilitate the effective implementation of civil rights policies, procedures, and institutional obligations. Identify operational deficiencies and recommend corrective measures, process improvements, and resource adjustments. Exercise appropriate administrative authority to coordinate compliance activities across University departments while maintaining established reporting relationships and institutional governance protocols. 2. Title IX Coordination and Administration Serve as the University's designated Title IX Coordinator and coordinate institutional compliance with applicable Title IX laws, regulations, and guidance. Oversee the receipt, assessment, documentation, and appropriate response to reports and complaints of sex discrimination, sexual harassment, sexual misconduct, dating violence, domestic violence, stalking, retaliation, and related conduct within Title IX's applicable scope. Coordinate the provision, implementation, and monitoring of supportive measures and other appropriate institutional responses. Oversee Title IX grievance procedures, investigations, informal resolution processes, hearings, appeals, and related activities as applicable under governing law and University policy. Ensure that Title IX processes are administered consistently, impartially, promptly, and in accordance with applicable procedural requirements. Identify and address actual or potential conflicts of interest and bias among individuals participating in Title IX processes. Coordinate with Student Affairs, Academic Affairs, Athletics, Human Resources, Campus Police, and other University departments regarding Title IX obligations and institutional responses. Monitor Title IX complaints and institutional data to identify patterns, systemic concerns, and potential compliance risks. Coordinate Title IX training, prevention, awareness, and educational initiatives. Maintain and periodically review Title IX policies, procedures, notices, and required institutional communications. Coordinate institutional responses to Title IX-related inquiries, investigations, audits, and compliance reviews. Provide timely updates to the Vice President and General Counsel regarding significant Title IX matters, regulatory developments, and institutional risks. 3. Title VI and Institutional Civil Rights Compliance Serve as the University's designated Title VI Coordinator and coordinate compliance with applicable federal civil rights requirements prohibiting discrimination based on race, color, or national origin. Oversee the receipt, assessment, investigation, and resolution of Title VI complaints, including matters involving legally recognized discrimination based on shared ancestry or ethnic characteristics. Coordinate institutional compliance assessments involving admissions, financial aid, scholarships, academic programs, athletics, student services, and other University programs and activities. Collaborate with appropriate University departments to identify and address potential discriminatory practices, barriers to participation, and systemic compliance concerns. Monitor institutional complaint data and identify trends requiring additional assessment, training, policy revisions, or corrective action. Coordinate the development and implementation of appropriate remedial measures and corrective action plans. Maintain required nondiscrimination notices, complaint procedures, and institutional communications. Coordinate responses to Title VI inquiries, investigations, and compliance reviews conducted by external regulatory agencies. Report significant Title VI compliance concerns and institutional risks to the Vice President and General Counsel. 4. ADA, Section 504, and Accessibility Services Provide senior operational oversight of the University's Accessibility Services function and related disability compliance programs. Supervise designated accessibility personnel and support the effective administration of student, employee, and visitor accessibility responsibilities. Coordinate institutional compliance efforts under the Americans with Disabilities Act, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, and other applicable disability laws. Oversee the administration and consistent application of reasonable accommodation policies and procedures within the Office's assigned responsibilities. Collaborate with Academic Affairs, Student Affairs, Human Resources, Facilities, Information Technology, and other departments regarding disability access and accommodation implementation. Ensure that appropriate procedures exist for reviewing accommodation requests, resolving accessibility concerns, and administering applicable reconsideration, grievance, and appeal processes. Monitor the timeliness, consistency, and documentation of accommodation determinations and implementation. Oversee the assessment and resolution of complaints alleging disability discrimination, failure to accommodate, retaliation, or other accessibility concerns. Coordinate institutional efforts to identify and address barriers affecting access to academic programs, employment, housing, facilities, transportation, events, and University services. Develop and monitor accessibility improvement initiatives and corrective action measures. Ensure appropriate coordination among designated ADA and Section 504 personnel while maintaining clearly defined operational responsibilities. Identify significant disability compliance risks and provide recommendations to the Vice President and General Counsel. 5. Digital Accessibility Compliance Coordinate institutional initiatives to achieve and maintain compliance with applicable digital accessibility requirements. Oversee accessibility assessments involving University websites, learning management systems, instructional technologies, electronic documents, web applications, digital communications, and other technology resources. Collaborate with Information Technology, Academic Affairs, Procurement, Marketing and Communications, and other departments regarding digital accessibility responsibilities. Support the development and implementation of digital accessibility policies, procedures, standards, remediation plans, and monitoring processes. Coordinate institutional assessments of digital accessibility barriers and recommend appropriate corrective actions. Monitor applicable federal and state digital accessibility requirements, including relevant technical standards and implementation deadlines. Provide operational updates and recommendations concerning digital accessibility compliance initiatives. 6. Investigations and Complaint Resolution Direct the administration of civil rights complaint intake, assessment, investigation, documentation, and resolution within the jurisdiction of the Office of Civil Rights. Conduct or oversee complex investigations involving discrimination, harassment, retaliation, accessibility, and other assigned civil rights matters. Establish investigative procedures and protocols designed to promote fairness, impartiality, thoroughness, consistency, and timely resolution. Assign investigators and other personnel based on the nature, complexity, and sensitivity of individual matters. Oversee investigative planning, evidence collection, witness interviews, documentation, findings, and reporting, as appropriate. Ensure appropriate separation of investigative, decision-making, and appellate responsibilities where required by applicable law or University policy. Establish procedures for identifying and addressing actual or potential conflicts of interest. Coordinate appropriate handling and escalation of complaints involving senior administrators, supervisors, faculty, employees, students, and other University community members. Maintain appropriate confidentiality and information-sharing practices consistent with applicable law and institutional requirements. Oversee the use of Maxient or comparable case management systems to document, monitor, and manage complaints. Evaluate complaint trends, investigative outcomes, and systemic concerns to identify opportunities for institutional improvement. Coordinate with the Vice President and General Counsel on significant, sensitive, high-risk, or legally complex matters. 7. Institutional Compliance Monitoring and Corrective Action Develop and maintain operational systems for assessing compliance with assigned civil rights obligations. Monitor implementation of corrective action plans, resolution agreements, remedial measures, and other institutional compliance commitments. Establish procedures for documenting responsible parties, implementation deadlines, required actions, and completion status. Coordinate with appropriate University departments to ensure timely implementation of assigned corrective measures. Evaluate the effectiveness of corrective actions and recommend additional measures when necessary. Identify recurring compliance deficiencies, operational gaps, and systemic institutional risks. Maintain documentation supporting compliance assessments, regulatory reporting, and institutional accountability. Provide periodic compliance monitoring reports and recommendations to the Vice President and General Counsel. 8. Employment Civil Rights Compliance and Human Resources Collaboration Collaborate with Human Resources regarding institutional compliance with Title VII, equal employment opportunity requirements, and other applicable employment-related civil rights laws. Coordinate, as assigned, the assessment and resolution of complaints involving workplace discrimination, harassment, retaliation, and related protected-class concerns. Support the development and implementation of employment-related nondiscrimination policies, training, and compliance initiatives. Coordinate with Human Resources and designated University officials regarding employee accommodation processes and workplace accessibility matters. Participate in employment-related investigations and compliance reviews as appropriate under established University protocols. Consult with the Vice President and General Counsel regarding employment matters involving significant legal, regulatory, or institutional risk. Maintain clear distinctions between the responsibilities of the Office of Civil Rights, Human Resources, and the Office of General Counsel. 9. Regulatory Affairs and External Agency Coordination Coordinate the operational preparation of institutional responses to civil rights inquiries, complaints, investigations, audits, compliance reviews, and information requests from appropriate governmental and regulatory agencies. Serve as an institutional point of contact for assigned civil rights regulatory matters, consistent with the oversight and direction of the Vice President and General Counsel. Coordinate with agencies including the U.S. Department of Education Office for Civil Rights, Equal Employment Opportunity Commission, U.S. Department of Justice, and other appropriate federal and state entities. Organize and review documentation, compliance reports, institutional records, and corrective action materials required for regulatory responses. Monitor applicable reporting obligations, regulatory deadlines, and institutional compliance commitments. Coordinate with the Office of General Counsel regarding legal strategy, litigation, privileged communications, significant regulatory exposure, and institutional legal positions. Monitor relevant legal and regulatory developments and recommend appropriate operational responses. 10. Campus Safety and Clery Act Coordination Collaborate with Campus Police, Student Affairs, and other designated University officials regarding matters where civil rights compliance responsibilities intersect with campus safety obligations. Coordinate appropriate information-sharing and institutional responses involving sexual misconduct, dating violence, domestic violence, stalking, and related matters. Support coordination between applicable Title IX responsibilities and institutional reporting, prevention, and notification obligations under the Clery Act and related requirements. Maintain appropriate coordination with the University's designated Clery Act administrators without assuming primary responsibility for Clery Act administration unless expressly assigned. 11. Policy Development, Training, and Prevention Coordinate the development, implementation, review, and revision of civil rights policies, procedures, protocols, and compliance resources. Ensure that Office of Civil Rights policies and procedures are consistent with applicable legal requirements and University governance processes. Develop and administer training and educational initiatives concerning discrimination, harassment, retaliation, Title IX, Title VI, accessibility, and related civil rights obligations. Coordinate mandatory and role-specific training for appropriate University personnel. Provide educational presentations and resources to faculty, staff, students, administrators, and other University stakeholders. Develop prevention and awareness initiatives designed to reduce compliance risks and promote institutional accountability. Evaluate training participation, effectiveness, and areas requiring additional education. Recommend policy and procedural improvements based on regulatory developments, complaint trends, and institutional assessments. 12. Records Management, Confidentiality, and Legal Preservation Establish and maintain appropriate procedures for secure records management, confidentiality, information access, and documentation within the Office of Civil Rights. Ensure compliance with applicable record retention, privacy, and information management requirements. Maintain accurate records of complaints, investigations, resolutions, accommodations, corrective actions, and regulatory activities. Coordinate with the Office of General Counsel regarding litigation holds, document preservation, subpoenas, regulatory investigations, and other legal processes. Ensure appropriate handling of sensitive information and legally protected records. Implement internal controls to support accurate documentation, secure information management, and appropriate access to institutional records. 13. Annual Institutional Civil Rights Compliance Assessment Prepare an annual institutional civil rights compliance assessment for review by the Vice President and General Counsel. Evaluate complaint activity, investigation trends, regulatory developments, and significant institutional compliance concerns. Assess training completion, prevention initiatives, accessibility programs, and corrective action implementation. Identify systemic risks, recurring deficiencies, operational challenges, and emerging compliance priorities. Evaluate Office of Civil Rights performance measures, resource utilization, staffing needs, and operational effectiveness. Develop recommendations for institutional improvements, policy revisions, additional training, and future compliance initiatives. Support the development of annual operational priorities based on assessment findings and executive direction. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Master’s degree from an accredited institution in Higher Education Administration, Public Administration, Compliance, Human Resources, Criminal Justice, Law, Business Administration, or a closely related field. At least seven (7) years of progressively responsible professional experience in higher education compliance, civil rights administration, investigations, accessibility services, legal affairs, human resources, student affairs, or a related field. Demonstrated knowledge of applicable federal and state civil rights laws and regulatory requirements. Significant experience conducting, managing, or supervising investigations involving discrimination, harassment, retaliation, accessibility, or related compliance matters. Demonstrated experience developing, implementing, or administering institutional policies, procedures, and complia"
    }
  },
